\title{Running Products and Primitive Parts:\\Mixed-Radix Uniqueness and Fibonacci Primitive Divisors}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 15, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Two forms of arithmetic novelty are studied by isolating contributions inherited from earlier stages. A mixed-radix system is defined from bases $b_i$ and running products $R_i=\prod_{j<i}b_j$. Valid digit strings have unique values, and the factorial number system is exactly the specialization $b_i=i+1$; factorial uniqueness therefore follows from general mixed-radix uniqueness. For Fibonacci numbers, strong divisibility reduces primitive-prime detection at index $n$ to exclusion at positive proper divisors of $n$. Repeated gcd stripping removes from $F_n$ all prime-power factors shared with Fibonacci numbers at those indices. A nontrivial survivor supplies a primitive prime. Exact finite evaluation of this criterion, combined with the prime-index case, proves that every $F_n$ with $13\le n\le 10000$ has a primitive prime divisor. Algorithms, complexity, applications, and the boundary between this finite theorem and the unbounded problem are discussed.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A positional numeral system separates an integer into layers. The lowest layer records a remainder, division removes it, and the operation repeats. A divisibility sequence has a related but richer layering: a term contains factors inherited from earlier terms, while a primitive factor appears for the first time.

This paper develops both ideas. In a general mixed-radix system the base may change from place to place. Accumulated products of the bases become place weights, and bounded digits have unique values. Choosing $b_i=i+1$ makes those weights equal to $i!$, so the factorial number system requires no separate uniqueness mechanism.

For the Fibonacci sequence, a primitive prime divisor of $F_n$ divides no earlier positive-index term. The identity
\[
\gcd(F_m,F_n)=F_{\gcd(m,n)}
\]
shows that non-primitivity can always be witnessed at a positive proper divisor of $n$. This suggests an explicit residual: remove from $F_n$ every prime power shared with $F_d$ for proper divisors $d$ of $n$. If anything greater than $1$ remains, it contains a primitive prime.

The resulting Fibonacci theorem is deliberately finite. The residual has been evaluated exactly for $13\le n\le10000$. No assertion beyond that endpoint is inferred from finite evaluation. This logical boundary identifies the missing ingredient for an unbounded result: a general lower bound for the primitive part.

\section{Mixed-radix positional systems}

\begin{definition}[Running products and values]
Let $b=(b_i)_{i\ge0}$ be a sequence of positive integers. Its running products are
\[
R_0=1,\qquad R_i=\prod_{j=0}^{i-1}b_j\quad(i>0).
\]
For a digit sequence $c=(c_i)_{i\ge0}$ and length $k$, define
\[
V_b(c;k)=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_iR_i.
\]
The string is \emph{valid through length $k$} if $0\le c_i<b_i$ for every $i<k$.
\end{definition}

Constant bases $b_i=B$ give $R_i=B^i$ and ordinary base-$B$ notation. Changing bases describe heterogeneous choices, including time units and combinatorial ranking schemes. The identity $R_{i+1}=b_iR_i$ is the central structural relation.

\begin{lemma}[Lower-block bound]\label{lem:lower}
If $c$ is valid through length $m$, then
\[
0\le V_b(c;m)<R_m.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $m$. The empty value is $0<R_0=1$. If the claim holds at $m$, then
\[
V_b(c;m+1)=V_b(c;m)+c_mR_m.
\]
Using $V_b(c;m)\le R_m-1$ and $c_m\le b_m-1$ gives
\[
V_b(c;m+1)\le(R_m-1)+(b_m-1)R_m=R_{m+1}-1.
\]
Thus all lower positions together cannot reach one unit of the next position.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mixed-Radix Uniqueness]\label{thm:unique}
Let $c$ and $d$ be valid through length $k$. If
\[
V_b(c;k)=V_b(d;k),
\]
then $c_i=d_i$ for every $i<k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Reduce the equality modulo $b_0$. Every $R_i$ with $i>0$ is divisible by $b_0$, while $R_0=1$, so $c_0\equiv d_0\pmod{b_0}$. Both digits belong to $[0,b_0)$ and hence are equal. Subtract their common contribution and divide by $b_0$. This produces the same problem for the shifted bases and digits, so iteration proves equality at every position.

Alternatively, take the greatest differing position $m$. Its discrepancy contributes at least $R_m$ in absolute value, whereas Lemma~\ref{lem:lower} bounds the total lower discrepancy by less than $R_m$. Higher contributions agree, giving a contradiction.
\end{proof}

When $b_i\ge2$, a value $N$ is decoded by repeated Euclidean division:
\[
c_i=N_i\bmod b_i,\qquad N_{i+1}=\left\lfloor N_i/b_i\right\rfloor,
\qquad N_0=N.
\]
The reconstruction invariant is
\[
N=\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}c_iR_i+N_tR_t.
\]
It follows inductively from $N_i=c_i+b_iN_{i+1}$. If $0\le N<R_k$, then $N_k=0$, so the extracted valid string represents $N$. Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}, this establishes a bijection between $[0,R_k)$ and valid strings of length $k$.

\section{The factorial specialization}

\begin{definition}[Factorial digits]
The factorial value of a length-$k$ digit string is
\[
V_!(c;k)=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_i i!.
\]
It is valid when $0\le c_i\le i$ for every $i<k$. In particular, $c_0=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Factorial--Mixed-Radix Bridge]\label{thm:bridge}
Set $b_i=i+1$. Then $R_i=i!$ for every $i$. Consequently,
\[
V_b(c;k)=V_!(c;k)
\]
for every $c$ and $k$, and mixed-radix validity is equivalent to factorial validity.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Directly,
\[
R_i=\prod_{j=0}^{i-1}(j+1)=1\cdot2\cdots i=i!.
\]
Substitution in the value sum proves value equality. For natural-number digits, $c_i<i+1$ is equivalent to $c_i\le i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness of Factorial Representations]\label{cor:factunique}
If $c$ and $d$ are valid factorial digit strings through length $k$ and
\[
\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_i i!=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}d_i i!,
\]
then $c_i=d_i$ for every $i<k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:bridge} turns the hypotheses into mixed-radix validity and value equality for $b_i=i+1$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
The integer $463$ has expansion
\[
463=0\cdot0!+1\cdot1!+0\cdot2!+1\cdot3!+4\cdot4!+3\cdot5!.
\]
Thus its low-to-high digits are $(0,1,0,1,4,3)$. Each coefficient obeys $c_i\le i$. Such digits rank permutations: the coefficient of $(m-1)!$ selects among $m$ objects, the next coefficient selects among the remaining $m-1$, and so forth. Uniqueness prevents collisions between valid selection histories.
\end{example}

\section{Fibonacci divisibility and first appearances}

\begin{definition}[Fibonacci sequence and primitive prime]
Let
\[
F_0=0,
\qquad F_1=1,
\qquad F_{n+2}=F_{n+1}+F_n.
\]
A prime $p$ is a \emph{primitive prime divisor} of $F_n$ if $p\mid F_n$ and $p\nmid F_k$ for every $0<k<n$. Its \emph{entry point}, when it exists, is the least positive $\alpha(p)$ such that $p\mid F_{\alpha(p)}$.
\end{definition}

Thus $p$ is primitive at $n$ precisely when $\alpha(p)=n$. The main structural input is the following classical identity.

\begin{theorem}[Strong Divisibility of Fibonacci Numbers]\label{thm:strong}
For all nonnegative integers $m,n$,
\[
\gcd(F_m,F_n)=F_{\gcd(m,n)}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Common-divisor descent]\label{lem:descent}
If $p\mid F_n$ and $p\mid F_k$, then $p\mid F_{\gcd(n,k)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The number $p$ divides $\gcd(F_n,F_k)$, which equals $F_{\gcd(n,k)}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:strong}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Proper-divisor witness]\label{lem:witness}
Let $n>0$, and suppose a prime $p$ divides $F_n$. If $p$ is not primitive at $n$, then some positive proper divisor $d$ of $n$ satisfies $p\mid F_d$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Non-primitivity supplies $k$ with $0<k<n$ and $p\mid F_k$. Put $d=\gcd(n,k)$. Then $d\mid n$, $d>0$, and $d\le k<n$. Lemma~\ref{lem:descent} gives $p\mid F_d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Proper-divisor reduction]\label{cor:reduction}
If a prime $p$ divides $F_n$ but divides no $F_d$ at a positive proper divisor $d$ of $n$, then $p$ is primitive at $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Prime-index case]\label{prop:prime}
If $n\ge3$ is prime, then $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inequality $F_n>1$ gives a prime factor $p$ of $F_n$. The only positive proper divisor of $n$ is $1$, and $F_1=1$. Hence $p\nmid F_1$, and Corollary~\ref{cor:reduction} makes $p$ primitive.
\end{proof}

\section{The primitive part}

\begin{definition}[Complete gcd stripping]
For positive integers $r,m$, repeatedly replace
\[
r\longleftarrow r/\gcd(r,m)
\]
while $\gcd(r,m)>1$. The process terminates because every nontrivial update strictly decreases $r$. Write $S(r,m)$ for the final survivor.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fibonacci primitive part]
Let $D(n)$ be the set of positive proper divisors of $n$. Begin with $r=F_n$ and, for every $d\in D(n)$, replace $r$ by $S(r,F_d)$. The final integer is denoted $\Phi(n)$ and called the primitive part of $F_n$ relative to proper divisor indices.
\end{definition}

Complete stripping removes the full multiplicity of every prime shared with the stripping argument. A fixed ordering of $D(n)$ is sufficient for all conclusions below.

\begin{lemma}[Stripping invariants]\label{lem:strip}
For positive $r,m$, one has $S(r,m)\mid r$ and
\[
\gcd(S(r,m),m)=1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every update divides the current value by one of its divisors, proving the first assertion. At termination the update condition fails, which is exactly the assertion that the gcd equals $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Primitive-part invariants]\label{lem:part}
For every positive $n$, the primitive part satisfies $\Phi(n)\mid F_n$. For every positive proper divisor $d$ of $n$,
\[
\gcd(\Phi(n),F_d)=1.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Repeatedly apply divisibility from Lemma~\ref{lem:strip}. At the stage for $d$, complete stripping makes the survivor coprime to $F_d$. Every later survivor divides that value, and divisors preserve coprimality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Primitive-Part Criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $n\ge3$. If $\Phi(n)>1$, then $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Choose a prime $p$ dividing $\Phi(n)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:part} puts $p$ in $F_n$ and excludes it from every $F_d$ at a positive proper divisor $d$ of $n$. Corollary~\ref{cor:reduction} then shows that $p$ is primitive.
\end{proof}

This criterion separates a general structural argument from a quantitative obligation: prove that the residual exceeds $1$.

\section{A finite primitive-divisor theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Composite indices in the finite range]\label{thm:composite}
If $n$ is composite and
\[
13\le n\le10000,
\]
then $F_n$ has a primitive prime divisor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exact exhaustive evaluation of the primitive-part algorithm at every composite index in the stated interval gives $\Phi(n)>1$. All operations are integer Fibonacci recurrence, divisibility, gcd, and exact division. Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} then supplies a primitive prime divisor.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Fibonacci Primitive-Divisor Theorem]\label{thm:finite}
For every integer $n$ satisfying
\[
13\le n\le10000,
\]
there exists a prime $p$ such that $p\mid F_n$ and
\[
p\nmid F_k\qquad\text{for every }0<k<n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $n$ is prime, apply Proposition~\ref{prop:prime}. If $n$ is composite, apply Theorem~\ref{thm:composite}. The two cases exhaust the interval.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The endpoint is part of the theorem]
The structural criterion is unbounded, but nontriviality of $\Phi(n)$ has here been established by finite evaluation only through $10000$. The upper bound cannot be removed without a new estimate. A natural stronger target is $\Phi(n)>n$ for every composite $n\ge13$, which would immediately imply the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion}.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\subsection{Mixed-radix extraction}
Given $N$ and bases $b_0,\ldots,b_{k-1}$ with $0\le N<R_k$, perform $k$ Euclidean divisions, recording $N\bmod b_i$ before replacing $N$ by $\lfloor N/b_i\rfloor$. Correctness is the reconstruction invariant from Section~2. With schoolbook arithmetic on an $L$-bit value, a conservative bit bound is $O(kL^2)$; fast division improves the arithmetic factor. The digit list and current quotient use $O(k+L)$ storage. Factorial extraction is the same algorithm with bases $1,2,3,\ldots$; the initial base-$1$ digit is forced to zero and may be inserted without division.

\subsection{Primitive-part extraction}
Compute $F_n$ and the $F_d$ for positive proper divisors $d$ of $n$. For each such $d$, repeatedly calculate $g=\gcd(r,F_d)$ and set $r\leftarrow r/g$ until $g=1$. Euclid's algorithm is polynomial in operand bit lengths. Each successful update at least halves $r$, so the number of successful steps is at most $\lfloor\log_2F_n\rfloor=O(n)$ per divisor, and usually much smaller because a whole gcd is removed at once.

Fast doubling computes $F_t$ in $O(\log t)$ big-integer multiplication stages. Proper divisors can be found using $O(\sqrt n)$ trial divisions, rather than scanning every smaller index. Full factorization of $F_n$ is unnecessary for stripping; only a nontrivial final survivor must be factored far enough to select a witness prime.

\subsection{Direct witness validation}
For modest $n$, a candidate $p$ can be checked directly against $F_1,\ldots,F_{n-1}$. This is less efficient than Corollary~\ref{cor:reduction}, but pedagogically transparent. The proper-divisor method replaces a history of $n-1$ terms by the usually much smaller divisor lattice $D(n)$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

Mixed-radix systems arise whenever position-dependent capacities are multiplied together: calendar arithmetic, angular units, tensor indexing, combinatorial ranking, and scheduling. Running products provide one correctness argument for all such encoders. The factorial specialization is particularly useful for permutation ranking and unranking because successive digit bounds match shrinking sets of available objects.

Primitive divisors measure the arrival of new arithmetic information in recurrence sequences. They are connected with multiplicative orders, periodicity modulo primes, and term factorization. The proper-divisor reduction changes the shape of the search: rather than asking whether a factor appeared anywhere in a long history, it asks whether the factor occurs on an index lattice governed by divisibility.

The conceptual parallel is residualization. In mixed radix, the current remainder is retained while division passes unresolved information upward. In the Fibonacci setting, gcd factors inherited from proper divisor indices are removed while the coprime residual retains new prime support. The objects differ, but both methods turn a global first-appearance question into a sequence of local eliminations controlled by an invariant.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The factorial bridge is exact and unrestricted. Every place-weight and validity identity follows from $R_i=i!$, and factorial uniqueness is a direct specialization of the general theorem.

The Fibonacci theorem has a different profile. Strong divisibility and the primitive-part criterion are general, whereas the interval arises in the proof of $\Phi(n)>1$ for composite indices. Finite computation must not be presented as an unbounded argument. This separation is productive because it states the missing theorem precisely.

Three directions follow. First, the extraction procedure can be developed uniformly for every base sequence satisfying $b_i\ge2$, giving an explicit bijection between $[0,R_k)$ and valid strings. Second, one may seek the inequality $\Phi(n)>n$ for all composite $n\ge13$, using golden-ratio growth of $F_n$ and upper bounds on inherited factors. Third, inherited prime powers should be controlled by a lifting-the-exponent law: if $\alpha(p)$ is the entry point of $p$ and $\alpha(p)\mid n$, the $p$-adic valuation of $F_n$ should be governed by the valuation at $F_{\alpha(p)}$ together with that of $n/\alpha(p)$, with appropriate treatment of exceptional primes and analogues for regular Lucas sequences.

\section{Conclusion}

The running product $R_i=\prod_{j<i}b_j$ contains the essential structure of positional notation. It yields the lower-block bound, uniqueness of valid mixed-radix strings, and the factorial system under $b_i=i+1$. In parallel, Fibonacci strong divisibility funnels every earlier occurrence of a prime factor down to a proper divisor index. Complete gcd stripping then creates a residual whose nontriviality guarantees a new prime.

These ingredients establish the precise statement that every $F_n$ with $13\le n\le10000$ has a primitive prime divisor. They also expose the frontier beyond the finite range: growth and valuation estimates strong enough to ensure that the primitive residual never disappears.

